'''''Cotoneaster coriaceus''''' (syn. ''Cotoneaster lacteus''), the '''late cotoneaster'''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=17 October 2014 }}</ref> or '''milkflower cotoneaster''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=COLA18|taxon=Cotoneaster lacteus|accessdate=17 January 2016 }}</ref> is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Rosaceae]], native to Tibet and south-central China.<ref name="POWO_722459-1" >{{cite POWO |id=722459-1 |title=''Cotoneaster coriaceus'' Franch. |access-date=25 April 2025 }}</ref> It is a large [[evergreen]] shrub growing to {{cvt|4|m}} tall and wide. Clusters of white flowers are followed by masses of small, globose, red fruits ([[pome]]s) in autumn.<ref>{{cite book |title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants |year=2008 |publisher=Dorling Kindersley |location=United Kingdom |isbn=978-1405332965 |pages=1136 }}</ref> Unusually for this genus, the fruits are avoided by birds, hence garden escapes are rare, and the fruit persists on the plant throughout the winter.<ref>{{cite web |last=Bourne |first=Val |title=How to grow: ''Cotoneaster lacteus'' |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/gardening/howtogrow/3316728/How-to-grow-Cotoneaster-lacteus.html |work=The Telegraph |publisher=TMG Ltd. |access-date=20 July 2013 |date=17 January 2004 }}</ref>

The Latin [[Botanical name#Binary name|specific epithet]] ''lacteus'' refers to the milk-white flowers,<ref name=RHSLG>{{cite book |last=Harrison |first=Lorraine |title=RHS Latin for gardeners |year=2012 |publisher=Mitchell Beazley |location=United Kingdom |isbn=9781845337315 |pages=224 }}</ref> and ''coriaceus'' refers to its leathery leaves.

''Cotoneaster coriaceus'' may be grown as a hedge. It has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/4629/Cotoneaster-lacteus/Details |title=''Cotoneaster lacteus'' |publisher=RHS |access-date=5 May 2020 |archive-date=20 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200920145451/https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/4629/Cotoneaster-lacteus/Details |url-status=live }}</ref>
